WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.872
This query rewrites the admission-count problem.

00:00:02.872 --> 00:00:06.712
The inner query groups admissions once by patient ID.

00:00:06.712 --> 00:00:10.522
The outer query joins those counts to patients.

00:00:10.522 --> 00:00:14.667
Run it and compare the result with the correlated scalar version.

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:18.902
The business meaning and patient-level grain should remain the same.

00:00:18.902 --> 00:00:23.502
When rewriting, always verify that the result did not change.

00:00:23.502 --> 00:00:28.362
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:28.362 --> 00:00:32.587
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:32.587 --> 00:00:36.782
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:36.782 --> 00:00:42.762
In a real setting such as a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up,

00:00:42.762 --> 00:00:48.767
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:50.882
before discussing syntax.

00:00:50.882 --> 00:00:56.960
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
